m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct a literature-informed candidate network using acetylene hydrocyanation, ammonia addition, cyanamide-dependent guanidination, reductive carbon-chain homologation, and aminonitrile chemistry. Ten distinct reaction sequences combine five chemically different guanidination stages with either direct aminonitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. These are ten candidate sequences, not ten experimentally demonstrated prebiotic pathways. The supplied review contains atom-unbalanced transformations and does not substantiate ten independent routes; its attribution of a complete arginine synthesis to Patel et al. (2015) is not treated as independently verified. Reducing equivalents, water, and sulfur coproducts are therefore made explicit, and substrate-specific extrapolations are labeled speculative. The three reported cyclization variants are not counted as separate pathways. Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox chemistry is used as a conditional reaction-class model rather than assuming elemental copper and sulfide automatically provide selective reduction. Conditions are proposed experimental windows, not reported optimized conditions. All stages are anoxic. Hydantoin branches require a separate, warmer hydrolysis environment because mild surface hydrolysis is kinetically problematic. Products are racemic: no L-selective process is established. Neutral molecular structures represent pH-dependent solution species; elemental sulfur is represented as S8 for redox bookkeeping, although polysulfides may also form. A null environment_formed denotes either an external feedstock or a molecule produced in more than one environment. The network contains 28 molecules and 26 reactions to represent the ten sequences without counting bookkeeping variants as independent chemistry.\n\nMOLECULES (28 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C2H2 (Acetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: CH2N2 (Cyanamide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_007: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_009: C3H3N (Acrylonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H6N2 (Beta-aminopropionitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C4H8N4 (2-Cyanoethylguanidine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C3H7NO (Beta-aminopropionaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H9N3O (Cyclic hemiaminal of 3-guanidinopropanal) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C4H8N2O (4-Amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H10N4O (First guanidino cyanohydrin) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C4H10N2OS (4-Amino-2-hydroxybutanethioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H12N4OS (Guanidino hydroxythioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C4H10N2S (4-Aminobutanethioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C5H12N4S (Guanidino butanethioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C4H9NO (Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C5H11N3O (4-Guanidinobutanal) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_022: C6H12N4O (Arginine-skeleton cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_023: C6H13N5 (Arginine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_024: C5H11N3 (Ornith