nitrile partial reduction. Hydantoin capture could provide a useful intermediate, but no stability or recovery advantage is demonstrated. Completion of rxn_024 under conditions that preserve arginine is less secure than the direct acid-hydrolysis endgame. The proposed circulation must also preserve the reactive aminonitrile during its first transfer and the hydantoin during subsequent processing.', 'The cyanoaldehyde route is structurally coherent and avoids the thioamide transformations. Its feasibility instead depends on selective reduction of one succinonitrile group, reductive amination without reducing the remaining nitrile or aldehyde to unwanted products, and another selective nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion. Sulfite can participate in photoredox chemistry, but the supplied material does not establish these particular transformations or their compatibility with aldehyde-bisulfite equilibria. The gamma-aminobutyronitrile cyclization problem remains. The final acid hydrolysis is plausible.', 'This pathway combines the unresolved selective photoreductions and reductive amination of P7 with an incompletely supported hydantoin endgame. All transformations conserve atoms and charge with the supplied coefficients, but that does not establish productive photochemistry or hydrolysis kinetics. Hydantoin formation is chemically plausible as a reaction class; selective release of arginine from N-alpha-carbamoylarginine remains a separate bottleneck. Surface-to-geothermal transfer is conceivable but lacks a quantified survival and dilution model.', 'Direct reductive guanidine alkylation is the defining weakness. Guanidine cannot simply be assumed to behave like ammonia in an aqueous reductive amination: protonation, resonance-delocalized nucleophilicity, condensation equilibria, and alkylation selectivity require specific support. Sulfite oxidation alone does not establish reduction of the necessary condensed intermediate. The pathway also retains uncertain cyanoaldehyde generation and guanidino-nitrile partial reduction. Avoiding free gamma-aminobutyronitrile removes one possible cyclization problem, but does not validate rxn_017.', 'The route is formally connected to arginine but combines poorly established direct guanidine reductive alkylation with selective nitrile photoreductions and an uncertain nonenzymatic decarbamoylation. Hydantoin capture does not resolve the upstream alkylation problem, and ring opening alone would leave the network at N-alpha-carbamoylarginine rather than the target. The proposed environmental separation is sensible as a hypothesis, but the necessary operating conditions and intermediate recoveries are not supplied.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, stereochemically unspecified; racemic product expected\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The supplied evidence does not justify ten chemically distinct, complete prebiotic pathways. Accordingly, this graph contains one explicitly conditional candidate rather than padding the pathway count with duplicate stoichiometries or unsupported alternatives. It corrects the review's atom balances, distinguishes reductive chemistry from hydrolysis, and represents cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion through carbonyl/imine equilibria rather than direct displacement of hydroxide. Early guanidination avoids requiring selective guanidination of ornithine or an unprotected four-carbon aminoaldehyde. Direct acid hydrolysis avoids the additional uncertainties of hydantoin decarbamoylation. The decisive reductions remain unvalidated: an explicit, hypothetical copper-consuming model closes their electron balances by forming Cu2S. This bookkeeping does not establish their thermodynamics, kinetics, or selectivity. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not experimental procedures established 