d\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H15N5O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-5-[(diaminomethylidene)amino]pentanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Argininamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-5-[(diaminomethylidene)amino]pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper(I)-assisted hydrocyanation of acetylene\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Copper(I) chloride/cyanide complexes in a sulfide-poor aqueous compartment\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar total pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I) complexes; active speciation must be established\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 3-6, dissolved Cu(I) 0.1-5 mM, HCN 1-20 mM, maintained acetylene supply, 1-72 h. Use a shaded, sulfide-poor compartment. Measure volatile losses and polymer formation; these are not reported optimized conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-assisted activation of acetylene permits net addition of HCN across its triple bond. This should not be represented as facile attack of cyanide on an intrinsically electrophilic, unactivated acetylene molecule.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review attributes this connection to Patel et al. (2015). Copper-assisted acetylene hydrocyanation is chemically recognizable, but the review supplies no reaction-level operating window. The proposed conditions therefore remain unvalidated. Sulfide must be excluded during this stage because copper-sulfide precipitation can remove the soluble copper species; subsequent sulfide chemistry cannot be assumed compatible in one pot.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 9.5-11, acrylonitrile 1-10 mM, ammonia 0.1-1 M, 6-168 h. Slow delivery of acrylonitrile into an ammonia-rich reservoir is a selectivity hypothesis, not an established geological concentration schedule.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds at the beta carbon of acrylonitrile, followed by proton transfer to give 3-aminopropanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the aza-Michael reaction described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). The reaction class is credible, but secondary cyanoethylation of the product amine and acrylonitrile polymerization compete. Ammonia excess may suppress multiple addition, although achievable concentrations and substrate-specific yields are not established by the supplied evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination with cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-365 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.5-10.5, aminonitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 5-50 mM, 1-14 days. Test shaded concentrating pools separately from sulfide-rich stages. Quantify cyanamide hydrolysis, oligomerization, and substrate cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The primary amine attacks the electrophilic carbon of cyanamide. Proton transfers produce the substituted guanidine without requiring a leaving group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review assigns this transformation to Patel et al. (2015), but does not provide substrate-specific kinetics or yields. Early guanidination is preferable within this candidate because it avoids the unresolved alpha-versus-side-chain selectivity of ornithine guanidination. It does not establish that cyanamide concentrations or reaction selectivity were sufficient on early Earth.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive hydrolysis and cyclization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; hypothetical screening range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 6-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, total dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM, copper loading 4-20 molar equivalents, 1-72 h. Compare dark controls with a laboratory 254 nm screen at 0.1-1 mW/cm2; that monochromatic test is not a sunlight-performance claim. Measure copper oxidation states, sulfide phases, passivation, and organic products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hypothesized two-electron reduction of the nitrile to an imine, hydrolysis to the corresponding aldehyde with ammonia release, and intramolecular hemiaminal formation. Formal closure requires two Cu atoms oxidized to Cu(I) in one Cu2S unit; H2S supplies sulfur and hydrogen, not an unspecified catalytic electron source.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE substrate-specific transformation. The review attributes Cu/H2S-assisted cyclic-product formation to Patel et al. (2015), but its listed equations omit the necessary reduction and sometimes the oxygen source. This balanced copper-consuming model is a proposed correction, not a verified mechanism from that paper. Sulfide trapping of copper is plausible, but does not demonstrate productive nitrile reduction. Passivation, overreduction, and competing hydrolysis remain decisive bottlenecks. The three review variants are not counted as separate pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide trapping through a cyclic-hemiaminal/open-aldehyde equilibrium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7-9, cyclic substrate 0.1-5 mM, HCN 5-50 mM, 1-48 h. Establish the open-chain aldehyde fraction experimentally. After the copper stage, sedimentation may remove solids but cannot be assumed to remove dissolved copper or recover the organic substrate quantitatively.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible opening of the cyclic hemiaminal exposes a three-carbon guanidino aldehyde. Cyanide addition to its carbonyl and proton transfer generate the cyanohydrin, adding one carbon from HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review describes ring opening under Patel et al. (2015). Carbonyl trapping through a ring-chain equilibrium is more defensible than direct cyanide displacement at a saturated hemiaminal carbon while retaining hydroxyl. Nevertheless, the equilibrium population, productive trapping rate, and substrate-specific selectivity are not established; this remains a conditional mechanistic interpretation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to the guanidino cyanohydrin nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, total dissolved sulfide 5-50 mM, 6-168 h. Use a shaded sulfide-bearing compartment and measure cyanohydrin dissociation, hydrolysis, and sulfur-containing side products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon. Proton transfers and tautomerization give the thioamide while retaining the adjacent hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile thiolysis is the transformation described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). Its reaction-class plausibility does not establish selectivity for this multifunctional substrate. Abundant Fe2+ can remove sulfide as iron sulfides, so sulfide availability must be measured rather than inferred from volcanic supply alone.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive deoxygenation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; hypothetical screening range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 6-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM, copper loading 4-20 equivalents, 1-72 h. Compare dark and 254 nm laboratory irradiation at 0.1-1 mW/cm2. Require measured organic mass balance, sulfur speciation, copper consumption, and passivation behavior before assigning productive conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The proposed net transformation replaces the alpha hydroxyl group with hydrogen and releases water. Two Cu atoms would supply two electrons by oxidation to Cu(I), with externally supplied sulfide captured as Cu2S. The elementary carbon-oxygen bond-cleavage mechanism is unresolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. The supplied review attributes reductive dehydroxylation to Patel et al. (2015), but gives no substrate-specific mechanistic or yield evidence sufficient to validate it here. This is reduction, not dehydration alone, and it neither branches nor lengthens the carbon skeleton. Explicit copper consumption avoids attributing reduction to an unbalanced equation or an undefined Cu/H2S catalytic cycle. Atom balance still does not establish that this reaction occurs selectively.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Unvalidated reductive desulfurization of the thioamide to an aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant and sulfur acceptor\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K; hypothetical screening range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 4-8, substrate 0.1-5 mM, copper loading 4-20 equivalents, 1-72 h. No additional sulfide is required by this model. Test aldehyde formation and survival independently before attempting downstream capture. Transfer time, dissolved-metal carryover, and intermediate recovery are unmeasured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hypothesized copper-mediated reductive sulfur removal gives an imine-level intermediate, followed by hydrolysis to the aldehyde and ammonia. Two Cu atoms become Cu(I) in Cu2S; the sulfur in this coproduct originates from the thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. The review's combined thioamide-to-cyanohydrin step, attributed to Patel et al. (2015), conceals aldehyde-level reduction before HCN addition. Splitting these operations exposes the missing chemistry. Unlike a model in which H2S cancels and substrate sulfur is implicitly oxidized to elemental sulfur, this model assigns the electrons to copper oxidation and retains sulfur at oxidation state -II. Neither this sulfur fate nor selective aldehyde formation is established. Hydrolysis, overreduction, copper passivation, and intramolecular trapping of the aldehyde must be excluded experimentally.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from 4-guanidinobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7-9, aldehyde 0.1-5 mM, HCN 5-50 mM, 0.5-24 h. Test capture immediately after aldehyde generation and compare with its independently measured decomposition and cyclization rates. No quantitative transfer recovery is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the accessible aldehyde carbonyl. Protonation of the alkoxide yields the five-carbon cyanohydrin backbone, with the guanidine carbon already present as the sixth carbon of the eventual target.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation is chemically conventional and is the trapping component of the combined transformation attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. Application to 4-guanidinobutanal is conditional on obtaining an accessible open-chain aldehyde. Guanidination avoids the free 4-aminobutanal/proline-precursor problem, but does not eliminate possible guanidine-assisted cyclization or intermolecular condensation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker equilibration from cyanohydrin to arginine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.5-10, cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total ammonia 0.1-1 M, 1-72 h. Maintain the cyanide-containing inventory while measuring volatilization, cyclic products, and aminonitrile formation. A shaded ammonia-rich stage is preferable to assuming compatibility with the preceding copper/sulfide mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin reversibly releases aldehyde and HCN. Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species from the aldehyde, and cyanide trapping gives the alpha-aminonitrile. The net reaction releases water; it is not direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a corrected Strecker interpretation of the amination attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. No general C-N-versus-C-O bond-strength argument establishes its direction or yield. Carbonyl availability, ammonia/cyanide speciation, and competing cyclization control productive flux. Without a demonstrated chiral influence, both configurations at the new alpha carbon are expected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of arginine aminonitrile to argininamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions from an acidic aqueous reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0-1.5, substrate 0.1-10 mM, 1-14 days. Use a separate shaded acidic geothermal pool after sulfide/metal treatment. Measure aminonitrile, amide, acid, and side-chain degradation as a time course; the stated window does not establish complete conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition and proton transfers to form the primary carboxamide. The guanidine and alpha-amino groups are predominantly protonated under these conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is the first part of the hydrolytic endgame described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). Resolving the amide makes the kinetic requirement explicit. Acidic hydrolysis is chemically credible, but the supplied evidence does not establish conversion rates or selectivity for this aminonitrile. Delivery into a sufficiently acidic hot pool, dilution losses, and survival during transfer remain environmental requirements rather than demonstrated facts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed argininamide hydrolysis to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions from an acidic aqueous reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-370 K; proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0-1, substrate 0.1-10 mM, 7-30 days, with time-resolved product analysis. These residence times are test windows, not measured completion times. Quantify guanidino-group retention and distinguish arginine from ornithine, other hydrolysis products, and residual amide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the acid-activated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia, present mainly as ammonium in the reaction medium, and forms the amino-acid carboxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis completes the nitrile-hydrolysis endgame attributed to Patel et al. (2015) by the supplied review. Together rxn_011 and rxn_012 consume two water molecules per aminonitrile without repeating molecule IDs in either reaction list. This conventional endpoint is preferable to assuming unverified hydantoin decarbamoylation, but selective completion below 370 K still requires experimental confirmation. The product is stereochemically unspecified arginine, not demonstrated enantiopure L-arginine.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Corrected early-guanidination candidate with explicit reductive bottlenecks\",\n      \"description\": \"A single connected candidate from acetylene, HCN, ammonia, cyanamide, water, sulfide, and native copper to racemic arginine. Early guanidination precedes two carbon-chain extensions, and the final aminonitrile is hydrolyzed through an explicit amide. Reactions rxn_004, rxn_007, and rxn_008 are unvalidated gatekeeper hypotheses, so this sequence is not an established viable synthesis. The hypothetical redox model requires a total of six Cu atoms converted to three Cu2S units per arginine molecule before accounting for losses; catalytic copper turnover is not assumed. Only one pathway is listed because the supplied evidence does not justify the requested ten chemically distinct routes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine, stereochemically unspecified; racemic product expected", "overall_strategy": "The supplied evidence does not justify ten chemically distinct, complete prebiotic pathways. Accordingly, this graph contains one explicitly conditional candidate rather than padding the pathway count with duplicate stoichiometries or unsupported alternatives. It corrects the review's atom balances, distinguishes reductive chemistry from hydrolysis, and represents cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion through carbonyl/imine equilibria rather than direct displacement of hydroxide. Early guanidination avoids requiring selective guanidination of ornithine or an unprotected four-carbon aminoaldehyde. Direct acid hydrolysis avoids the additional uncertainties of hydantoin decarbamoylation. The decisive reductions remain unvalidated: an explicit, hypothetical copper-consuming model closes their electron balances by forming Cu2S. This bookkeeping does not establish their thermodynamics, kinetics, or selectivity. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not experimental procedures established by Patel et al. (2015). Neither a demonstrated complete synthesis nor ten validated routes can be claimed from the supplied review. Neutral molecular formulas are used for bookkeeping; aqueous protonation states and catalytic proton transfers are implicit. The schema lacks a coproduct role, so Cu2S is classified as an intermediate although it is a terminal inorganic coproduct.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "Ethyne", "common_name": "Acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "Cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "Cu", "iupac_name": "Copper", "common_name": "Native copper", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Cu", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "Cu2S", "iupac_name": "Copper(I) sulfide", "common_name": "Chalcocite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "Prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "Acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-Aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "Beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8N4", "iupac_name": "3-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]propanenitrile", "common_name": "N-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8N4/c5-2-1-3-8-4(6)7/h1,3H2,(H4,6,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H9N3O", "iupac_name": "2-Imino-1,3-diazinan-4-ol", "common_name": "4-Hydroxy-2-iminohexahydropyrimidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9N3O/c5-4-6-2-1-3(8)7-4/h3,8H,1-2H2,(H3,5,6,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H10N4O", "iupac_name": "4-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]-2-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Four-carbon guanidino cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10N4O/c6-3-4(10)1-2-9-5(7)8/h4,10H,1-2H2,(H4,7,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H12N4OS", "iupac_name": "4-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]-2-hydroxybutanethioamide", "common_name": "Alpha-hydroxy guanidino thioamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H12N4OS/c6-4(11)3(10)1-2-9-5(7)8/h3,10H,1-2H2,(H2,6,11)(H4,7,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H12N4S", "iupac_name": "4-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]butanethioamide", "common_name": "4-Guanidinobutanethioamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H12N4S/c6-4(10)2-1-3-9-5(7)8/h1-3H2,(H2,6,10)(H4,7,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H11N3O", "iupac_name": "4-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]butanal", "common_name": "4-Guanidinobutanal", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H12N4O", "iupac_name": "5-[(Diaminomethylidene)amino]-2-hydroxypentanenitrile", "common_name": "Five-carbon guanidino cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H12N4O/c7-4-5(11)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h5,11H,1-3H2,(H4,8,9,10)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H13N5", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-[(diaminomethylidene)amino]pentanenitrile", "common_name": "Arginine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H13N5/c7-4-5(8)2-1-3-11-6(9)10/h5H,1-3,8H2,(H4,9,10,11)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H15N5O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-[(diaminomethylidene)amino]pentanamide", "common_name": "Argininamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-[(diaminomethylidene)amino]pentanoic acid", "common_name": "Arginine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Copper(I)-assisted hydrocyanation of acetylene", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Copper(I) chloride/cyanide complexes in a sulfide-poor aqueous compartment"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar total pressure", "catalyst": "Copper(I) complexes; active speciation must be established", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 3-6, dissolved Cu(I) 0.1-5 mM, HCN 1-20 mM, maintained acetylene supply, 1-72 h. Use a shaded, sulfide-poor compartment. Measure volatile losses and polymer formation; these are not reported optimized conditions."}, "mechanism": "Metal-assisted activation of acetylene permits net addition of HCN across its triple bond. This should not be represented as facile attack of cyanide on an intrinsically electrophilic, unactivated acetylene molecule.", "reasoning": "The supplied review attributes this connection to Patel et al. (2015). Copper-assisted acetylene hydrocyanation is chemically recognizable, but the review supplies no reaction-level operating window. The proposed conditions therefore remain unvalidated. Sulfide must be excluded during this stage because copper-sulfide precipitation can remove the soluble copper species; subsequent sulfide chemistry cannot be assumed compatible in one pot."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 9.5-11, acrylonitrile 1-10 mM, ammonia 0.1-1 M, 6-168 h. Slow delivery of acrylonitrile into an ammonia-rich reservoir is a selectivity hypothesis, not an established geological concentration schedule."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds at the beta carbon of acrylonitrile, followed by proton transfer to give 3-aminopropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the aza-Michael reaction described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). The reaction class is credible, but secondary cyanoethylation of the product amine and acrylonitrile polymerization compete. Ammonia excess may suppress multiple addition, although achievable concentrations and substrate-specific yields are not established by the supplied evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Early guanidination with cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-365 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.5-10.5, aminonitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 5-50 mM, 1-14 days. Test shaded concentrating pools separately from sulfide-rich stages. Quantify cyanamide hydrolysis, oligomerization, and substrate cyclization."}, "mechanism": "The primary amine attacks the electrophilic carbon of cyanamide. Proton transfers produce the substituted guanidine without requiring a leaving group.", "reasoning": "The supplied review assigns this transformation to Patel et al. (2015), but does not provide substrate-specific kinetics or yields. Early guanidination is preferable within this candidate because it avoids the unresolved alpha-versus-side-chain selectivity of ornithine guanidination. It does not establish that cyanamide concentrations or reaction selectivity were sufficient on early Earth."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive hydrolysis and cyclization", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006", "mol_005", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; hypothetical screening range only", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 6-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, total dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM, copper loading 4-20 molar equivalents, 1-72 h. Compare dark controls with a laboratory 254 nm screen at 0.1-1 mW/cm2; that monochromatic test is not a sunlight-performance claim. Measure copper oxidation states, sulfide phases, passivation, and organic products."}, "mechanism": "Hypothesized two-electron reduction of the nitrile to an imine, hydrolysis to the corresponding aldehyde with ammonia release, and intramolecular hemiaminal formation. Formal closure requires two Cu atoms oxidized to Cu(I) in one Cu2S unit; H2S supplies sulfur and hydrogen, not an unspecified catalytic electron source.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE substrate-specific transformation. The review attributes Cu/H2S-assisted cyclic-product formation to Patel et al. (2015), but its listed equations omit the necessary reduction and sometimes the oxygen source. This balanced copper-consuming model is a proposed correction, not a verified mechanism from that paper. Sulfide trapping of copper is plausible, but does not demonstrate productive nitrile reduction. Passivation, overreduction, and competing hydrolysis remain decisive bottlenecks. The three review variants are not counted as separate pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanide trapping through a cyclic-hemiaminal/open-aldehyde equilibrium", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7-9, cyclic substrate 0.1-5 mM, HCN 5-50 mM, 1-48 h. Establish the open-chain aldehyde fraction experimentally. After the copper stage, sedimentation may remove solids but cannot be assumed to remove dissolved copper or recover the organic substrate quantitatively."}, "mechanism": "Reversible opening of the cyclic hemiaminal exposes a three-carbon guanidino aldehyde. Cyanide addition to its carbonyl and proton transfer generate the cyanohydrin, adding one carbon from HCN.", "reasoning": "The supplied review describes ring opening under Patel et al. (2015). Carbonyl trapping through a ring-chain equilibrium is more defensible than direct cyanide displacement at a saturated hemiaminal carbon while retaining hydroxyl. Nevertheless, the equilibrium population, productive trapping rate, and substrate-specific selectivity are not established; this remains a conditional mechanistic interpretation."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Sulfide addition to the guanidino cyanohydrin nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, total dissolved sulfide 5-50 mM, 6-168 h. Use a shaded sulfide-bearing compartment and measure cyanohydrin dissociation, hydrolysis, and sulfur-containing side products."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon. Proton transfers and tautomerization give the thioamide while retaining the adjacent hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Nitrile thiolysis is the transformation described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). Its reaction-class plausibility does not establish selectivity for this multifunctional substrate. Abundant Fe2+ can remove sulfide as iron sulfides, so sulfide availability must be measured rather than inferred from volcanic supply alone."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive deoxygenation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_006", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; hypothetical screening range only", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 6-9, substrate 0.1-5 mM, dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM, copper loading 4-20 equivalents, 1-72 h. Compare dark and 254 nm laboratory irradiation at 0.1-1 mW/cm2. Require measured organic mass balance, sulfur speciation, copper consumption, and passivation behavior before assigning productive conversion."}, "mechanism": "The proposed net transformation replaces the alpha hydroxyl group with hydrogen and releases water. Two Cu atoms would supply two electrons by oxidation to Cu(I), with externally supplied sulfide captured as Cu2S. The elementary carbon-oxygen bond-cleavage mechanism is unresolved.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. The supplied review attributes reductive dehydroxylation to Patel et al. (2015), but gives no substrate-specific mechanistic or yield evidence sufficient to validate it here. This is reduction, not dehydration alone, and it neither branches nor lengthens the carbon skeleton. Explicit copper consumption avoids attributing reduction to an unbalanced equation or an undefined Cu/H2S catalytic cycle. Atom balance still does not establish that this reaction occurs selectively."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Unvalidated reductive desulfurization of the thioamide to an aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native copper, represented by mol_007, as a hypothetical stoichiometric reductant and sulfur acceptor"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K; hypothetical screening range only", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 4-8, substrate 0.1-5 mM, copper loading 4-20 equivalents, 1-72 h. No additional sulfide is required by this model. Test aldehyde formation and survival independently before attempting downstream capture. Transfer time, dissolved-metal carryover, and intermediate recovery are unmeasured."}, "mechanism": "Hypothesized copper-mediated reductive sulfur removal gives an imine-level intermediate, followed by hydrolysis to the aldehyde and ammonia. Two Cu atoms become Cu(I) in Cu2S; the sulfur in this coproduct originates from the thioamide.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. The review's combined thioamide-to-cyanohydrin step, attributed to Patel et al. (2015), conceals aldehyde-level reduction before HCN addition. Splitting these operations exposes the missing chemistry. Unlike a model in which H2S cancels and substrate sulfur is implicitly oxidized to elemental sulfur, this model assigns the electrons to copper oxidation and retains sulfur at oxidation state -II. Neither this sulfur fate nor selective aldehyde formation is established. Hydrolysis, overreduction, copper passivation, and intramolecular trapping of the aldehyde must be excluded experimentally."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation from 4-guanidinobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7-9, aldehyde 0.1-5 mM, HCN 5-50 mM, 0.5-24 h. Test capture immediately after aldehyde generation and compare with its independently measured decomposition and cyclization rates. No quantitative transfer recovery is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the accessible aldehyde carbonyl. Protonation of the alkoxide yields the five-carbon cyanohydrin backbone, with the guanidine carbon already present as the sixth carbon of the eventual target.", "reasoning": "Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation is chemically conventional and is the trapping component of the combined transformation attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. Application to 4-guanidinobutanal is conditional on obtaining an accessible open-chain aldehyde. Guanidination avoids the free 4-aminobutanal/proline-precursor problem, but does not eliminate possible guanidine-assisted cyclization or intermolecular condensation."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Strecker equilibration from cyanohydrin to arginine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.5-10, cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total ammonia 0.1-1 M, 1-72 h. Maintain the cyanide-containing inventory while measuring volatilization, cyclic products, and aminonitrile formation. A shaded ammonia-rich stage is preferable to assuming compatibility with the preceding copper/sulfide mixture."}, "mechanism": "The cyanohydrin reversibly releases aldehyde and HCN. Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species from the aldehyde, and cyanide trapping gives the alpha-aminonitrile. The net reaction releases water; it is not direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is a corrected Strecker interpretation of the amination attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. No general C-N-versus-C-O bond-strength argument establishes its direction or yield. Carbonyl availability, ammonia/cyanide speciation, and competing cyclization control productive flux. Without a demonstrated chiral influence, both configurations at the new alpha carbon are expected."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of arginine aminonitrile to argininamide", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from an acidic aqueous reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0-1.5, substrate 0.1-10 mM, 1-14 days. Use a separate shaded acidic geothermal pool after sulfide/metal treatment. Measure aminonitrile, amide, acid, and side-chain degradation as a time course; the stated window does not establish complete conversion."}, "mechanism": "Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition and proton transfers to form the primary carboxamide. The guanidine and alpha-amino groups are predominantly protonated under these conditions.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydration is the first part of the hydrolytic endgame described in the supplied review under Patel et al. (2015). Resolving the amide makes the kinetic requirement explicit. Acidic hydrolysis is chemically credible, but the supplied evidence does not establish conversion rates or selectivity for this aminonitrile. Delivery into a sufficiently acidic hot pool, dilution losses, and survival during transfer remain environmental requirements rather than demonstrated facts."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Acid-catalyzed argininamide hydrolysis to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from an acidic aqueous reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K; proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0-1, substrate 0.1-10 mM, 7-30 days, with time-resolved product analysis. These residence times are test windows, not measured completion times. Quantify guanidino-group retention and distinguish arginine from ornithine, other hydrolysis products, and residual amide."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the acid-activated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia, present mainly as ammonium in the reaction medium, and forms the amino-acid carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis completes the nitrile-hydrolysis endgame attributed to Patel et al. (2015) by the supplied review. Together rxn_011 and rxn_012 consume two water molecules per aminonitrile without repeating molecule IDs in either reaction list. This conventional endpoint is preferable to assuming unverified hydantoin decarbamoylation, but selective completion below 370 K still requires experimental confirmation. The product is stereochemically unspecified arginine, not demonstrated enantiopure L-arginine."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Corrected early-guanidination candidate with explicit reductive bottlenecks", "description": "A single connected candidate from acetylene, HCN, ammonia, cyanamide, water, sulfide, and native copper to racemic arginine. Early guanidination precedes two carbon-chain extensions, and the final aminonitrile is hydrolyzed through an explicit amide. Reactions rxn_004, rxn_007, and rxn_008 are unvalidated gatekeeper hypotheses, so this sequence is not an established viable synthesis. The hypothetical redox model requires a total of six Cu atoms converted to three Cu2S units per arginine molecule before accounting for losses; catalytic copper turnover is not assumed. Only one pathway is listed because the supplied evidence does not justify the requested ten chemically distinct routes.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 414442.9597854614, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:04:47.165189", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine, stereochemically unspecified; racemic product expected\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The supplied evidence does not justify ten chemically distinct, complete prebiotic pathways. Accordingly, this graph contains one explicitly conditional candidate rather than padding the pathway count with duplicate stoichiometries or unsupported alternatives. It corrects the review's atom balances, distinguishes reductive chemistry from hydrolysis, and represents cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion through carbonyl/imine equilibria rather than direct displacement of hydroxide. Early guanidination avoids requiring selective guanidination of ornithine or an unprotected four-carbon aminoaldehyde. Direct acid hydrolysis avoids the additional uncertainties of hydantoin decarbamoylation. The decisive reductions remain unvalidated: an explicit, hypothetical copper-consuming model closes their electron balances by forming Cu2S. This bookkeeping does not establish their thermodynamics, kinetics, or selectivity. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not experimental procedures established by Patel et al. (2015). Neither a demonstrated complete synthesis nor ten validated routes can be claimed from the supplied review. Neutral molecular formulas are used for bookkeeping; aqueous protonation states and catalytic proton transfers are implicit. The schema lacks a coproduct role, so Cu2S is classified as an intermediate although it is a terminal inorganic coproduct.\n\nMOLECULES (20 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C2H2 (Acetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_002: HCN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: CH2N2 (Cyanamide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_007: Cu (Native copper) [starting]\n  - mol_008: Cu2S (Chalcocite) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H3N (Acrylonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H6N2 (Beta-aminopropionitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C4H8N4 (N-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H9N3O (4-Hydroxy-2-iminohexahydropyrimidine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H10N4O (Four-carbon guanidino cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H12N4OS (Alpha-hydroxy guanidino thioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H12N4S (4-Guanidinobutanethioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C5H11N3O (4-Guanidinobutanal) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C6H12N4O (Five-carbon guanidino cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C6H13N5 (Arginine aminonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C6H15N5O (Argininamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C6H14N4O2 (Arginine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_009 (Copper(I)-assisted hydrocyanation of acetylene) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_010 (Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_010, mol_004 -> mol_011 (Early guanidination with cyanamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_011, mol_006, mol_005, mol_007 -> mol_012, mol_003, mol_008 (Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive hydrolysis and cyclization) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_012, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Cyanide trapping through a cyclic-hemiaminal/open-aldehyde equilibrium) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_013, mol_005 -> mol_014 (Sulfide addition to the guanidino cyanohydrin nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_014, mol_005, mol_007 -> mol_015, mol_006, mol_008 (Unvalidated copper-consuming reductive deoxygenation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_015, mol_006, mol_007 -> mol_016, mol_003, mol_008 (Unvalidated reductive desulfurization of the thioamide to an aldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_016, mol_002 -> mol_017 (Cyanohydrin formation from 4-guanidinobutanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_018, mol_006 (Strecker equilibration from cyanohydrin to arginine aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_018, mol_006 -> mol_019 (Acid-catalyzed hydration of arginine aminonitrile to argininamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_019, mol_006 -> mol_020, mol_003 (Acid-catalyzed argininamide hydrolysis to arginine) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (1):\n  - P1: Corrected early-guanidination candidate with explicit reductive bottlenecks (12 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 9 balanced as written | 3 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: C4H8N4 + H2O + H2S + 2 Cu -> C4H9N3O + NH3 + Cu2S\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: C5H12N4OS + H2S + 2 Cu -> C5H12N4S + H2O + Cu2S\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C5H12N4S + H2O + 2 Cu -> C5H11N3O + NH3 + Cu2S\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Arginine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nOne experimentally demonstrated pathway produces L-arginine (C6H14N4O2, SMILES: C(CC(C(=O)O)N)CN=C(N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10), MW: 174.20) from prebiotically available precursors. The entire pathway operates within the cyanosulfidic (Surface) environment.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-arginine through a 10-step sequence beginning from acetylene, hydrogen cyanide, ammonia, cyanamide, and hydrogen sulfide. The pathway proceeds through nitrile intermediates, guanidine formation, a cyclic pyrimidine intermediate, thioamide chemistry, and final nitrile hydrolysis. Elemental copper and hydrogen sulfide serve as key reagents in several steps (cyclization, dehydroxylation, thioamide-to-nitrile conversion).\n\n**Step 1**: Acetylene + Hydrogen cyanide \u2192 Acrylonitrile\n- SMILES: C#N.C#C>>C=CC#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Addition of HCN across the triple bond of acetylene, forming the vinyl nitrile (acrylonitrile). This is a conjugate addition reaction where the cyanide ion acts as a nucleophile attacking the electrophilic acetylenic carbon.\n\n**Step 2**: Acrylonitrile + Ammonia \u2192 3-Aminopropanenitrile\n- SMILES: C=CC#N.N>>N#CCCN\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Conjugate (Michael) addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile. The nucleophilic nitrogen of NH3 attacks the \u03b2-carbon of the \u03b1,\u03b2-unsaturated nitrile in a 1,4-addition, yielding the \u03b2-amino nitrile (3-aminopropanenitrile).\n\n**Step 3**: 3-Aminopropanenitrile + Cyanamide \u2192 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine\n- SMILES: N#CCCN.N#CN>>N#CCCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: The primary amine of 3-aminopropanenitrile attacks the electrophilic carbon of cyanamide (H2N-C\u2261N), forming a guanidine linkage. This nucleophilic addition converts the nitrile of cyanamide into an amidine (guanidine) group, introducing the characteristic N=C(NH2)-NH motif of arginine's side chain.\n\n**Step 4**: 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine \u2192 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine\n- Three variant conditions were reported, all employing elemental copper and hydrogen sulfide as reagents:\n- **Variant A** (dry cyclization): 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine\n  - SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1\n  - Agents: Elemental copper (Cu), hydrogen sulfide (H2S)\n- **Variant B** (hydrolytic cyclization): 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine + H2O \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine\n  - SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N.O>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1\n  - Agents: Elemental copper (Cu), hydrogen sulfide (H2S)\n- **Variant C** (hydrolytic cyclization with NH3 release): 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine + H2O \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine + NH3\n  - SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N.O>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1.N\n  - Agents: Elemental copper (Cu), hydrogen sulfide (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Intramolecular cyclization \u2014 the nitrile group of 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine undergoes nucleophilic attack by the terminal guanidine nitrogen, forming a six-membered pyrimidine ring. Water participates in the hydrolysis of the nitrile to a hydroxyl group at the C4 position. Copper and H2S facilitate the reduction/cyclization under cyanosulfidic conditions. The three variants represent different stoichiometries of the same fundamental cyclization process.\n\n**Step 5**: 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine + Hydrogen cyanide \u2192 Amino((3-cyano-3-hydroxypropyl)amino)methaniminium\n- SMILES: NC1=NCCC(O)N1.C#N>>N#CC(O)CCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Ring-opening of the pyrimidine by HCN \u2014 hydrogen cyanide attacks the C4 position of the cyclic hemiaminal, opening the six-membered ring and introducing a cyano group. The hydroxyl group is retained at the carbon bearing the new nitrile, yielding an open-chain \u03b1-cyanohydrin-guanidine intermediate.\n\n**Step 6**: Amino((3-cyano-3-hydroxypropyl)amino)methaniminium + Hydrogen sulfide \u2192 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide\n- SMILES: N#CC(O)CCNC(=N)N.S>>N=C(N)NCCC(O)C(N)=S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Thiolysis of the nitrile group \u2014 H2S adds across the C\u2261N triple bond of the cyano group, converting it to a thioamide (C(=S)NH2). This is the characteristic cyanosulfidic transformation where hydrogen sulfide acts as a nucleophile on the nitrile carbon, analogous to hydrolysis but yielding a thioamide instead of an amide.\n\n**Step 7**: 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide \u2192 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)butanethioamide + H2O\n- SMILES: N=C(N)NCCC(O)C(N)=S>>N=C(N)NCCCC(N)=S.O\n- Agents: Elemental copper (Cu), hydrogen sulfide (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Reductive dehydroxylation \u2014 the hydroxyl group adjacent to the thioamide is eliminated as water. Copper and H2S provide the reducing equivalents needed for this deoxygenation step. The carbon skeleton is homologated from a branched (hydroxyl-bearing) to a linear chain.\n\n**Step 8**: 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)butanethioamide + H2O + HCN \u2192 2-(4-Cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine + H2S + NH3\n- SMILES: N=C(N)NCCCC(N)=S.O.[C-]#N>>N#CC(O)CCCNC(=N)N.S.N\n- Agents: Elemental copper (Cu), hydrogen sulfide (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Thioamide-to-nitrile conversion with concomitant hydroxylation \u2014 HCN and water participate in a complex exchange where the thioamide sulfur is displaced (released as H2S), the nitrogen is lost as ammonia, and a new cyanohydrin is formed. This effectively converts the C(=S)NH2 terminus back to a C(OH)(C\u2261N) functionality, extending the oxidation state cycling that is characteristic of cyanosulfidic chemistry.\n\n**Step 9**: 2-(4-Cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine + Ammonia \u2192 2-(4-Amino-4-cyanobutyl)guanidine\n- SMILES: N#CC(O)CCCNC(=N)N.N>>N#CC(N)CCCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Amination \u2014 the hydroxyl group at C4 is displaced by ammonia in a substitution reaction, replacing -OH with -NH2. This introduces the \u03b1-amino group that will become the amino acid amine in the final product. The cyano group is retained for subsequent hydrolysis.\n\n**Step 10**: 2-(4-Amino-4-cyanobutyl)guanidine + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Arginine + NH3\n- SMILES: N#CC(N)CCCNC(=N)N.O.O>>N.N=C(N)NCCCC(N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 the terminal nitrile group undergoes stepwise hydrolysis by water, first forming an amide intermediate, then the carboxylic acid, with release of ammonia. This converts the \u03b1-aminonitrile to the \u03b1-amino acid (arginine). This is the same Strecker-type nitrile hydrolysis mechanism seen in the synthesis of other amino acids (alanine, serine, glycine) under cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine (C4H8N4)\n- Formula: C4H8N4\n- SMILES: C(CN=C(N)N)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N4/c5-2-1-3-8-4(6)7/h1,3H2,(H4,6,7,8)\n- MW: 112.13\n- Role: **Key branch-point intermediate** \u2014 this is the molecule where the guanidine side chain has been established and that undergoes intramolecular cyclization to the pyrimidine ring. It is the substrate for three variant cyclization reactions (with Cu/H2S), making it a convergence/divergence point in the network.\n\n### Hub Molecule: 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine (C4H9N3O)\n- Formula: C4H9N3O\n- SMILES: C1CN=C(NC1O)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9N3O/c5-4-6-2-1-3(8)7-4/h3,8H,1-2H2,(H3,5,6,7)\n- MW: 115.13\n- Role: **Cyclic intermediate** \u2014 the six-membered pyrimidine ring formed by cyclization of 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine. This ring is subsequently opened by HCN to extend the carbon chain. It is the product of three variant cyclization reactions (convergence point) and the substrate for ring-opening (Step 5).\n\n### Intermediate Molecules\n\n**Acrylonitrile** (C3H3N)\n- Formula: C3H3N\n- SMILES: C=CC#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\n- MW: 53.06\n- Role: Vinyl nitrile intermediate \u2014 formed from acetylene + HCN, serves as Michael acceptor for ammonia addition.\n\n**3-Aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2)\n- Formula: C3H6N2\n- SMILES: C(CN)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2\n- MW: 70.09\n- Role: \u03b2-Amino nitrile intermediate \u2014 formed from acrylonitrile + NH3, reacts with cyanamide to form the guanidine linkage.\n\n**Amino((3-cyano-3-hydroxypropyl)amino)methaniminium** (C5H10N4O)\n- Formula: C5H10N4O\n- SMILES: C(CN=C(N)N)C(C#N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H10N4O/c6-3-4(10)1-2-9-5(7)8/h4,10H,1-2H2,(H4,7,8,9)\n- MW: 142.16\n- Role: Ring-opened cyanohydrin-guanidine intermediate \u2014 formed by HCN opening of the pyrimidine ring. Contains both the nitrile and hydroxyl groups that undergo subsequent cyanosulfidic transformations.\n\n**4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide** (C5H12N4OS)\n- Formula: C5H12N4OS\n- SMILES: C(CN=C(N)N)C(C(=S)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H12N4OS/c6-4(11)3(10)1-2-9-5(7)8/h3,10H,1-2H2,(H2,6,11)(H4,7,8,9)\n- MW: 176.24\n- Role: Thioamide intermediate \u2014 formed by H2S thiolysis of the nitrile. The hydroxyl group is subsequently removed by Cu/H2S-mediated dehydroxylation.\n\n**4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)butanethioamide** (C5H12N4S)\n- Formula: C5H12N4S\n- SMILES: C(CC(=S)N)CN=C(N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H12N4S/c6-4(10)2-1-3-9-5(7)8/h1-3H2,(H2,6,10)(H4,7,8,9)\n- MW: 160.24\n- Role: Dehydroxylated thioamide intermediate \u2014 formed by Cu/H2S-mediated removal of the hydroxyl group. The thioamide is subsequently converted back to a cyanohydrin by HCN exchange.\n\n**2-(4-Cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine** (C6H12N4O)\n- Formula: C6H12N4O\n- SMILES: C(CC(C#N)O)CN=C(N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H12N4O/c7-4-5(11)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h5,11H,1-3H2,(H4,8,9,10)\n- MW: 156.19\n- Role: Cyanohydrin-guanidine intermediate \u2014 formed by thioamide-to-nitrile conversion. The hydroxyl group is displaced by ammonia in the next step to form the \u03b1-amino group.\n\n**2-(4-Amino-4-cyanobutyl)guanidine** (C6H13N5)\n- Formula: C6H13N5\n- SMILES: C(CC(C#N)N)CN=C(N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H13N5/c7-4-5(8)2-1-3-11-6(9)10/h5H,1-3,8H2,(H4,9,10,11)\n- MW: 155.20\n- Role: \u03b1-Aminonitrile precursor \u2014 the direct precursor to arginine. Contains the complete carbon skeleton with \u03b1-amino group and guanidine side chain; only the nitrile-to-carboxylic acid hydrolysis remains.\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **Acetylene** (C2H2): SMILES: C#C, InChI: InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H, MW: 26.04\n- **Hydrogen cyanide** (HCN): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03\n- **Ammonia** (NH3): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03\n- **Cyanamide** (CH2N2): SMILES: C(#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2, MW: 42.04\n- **Hydrogen sulfide** (H2S): SMILES: S, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2, MW: 34.08\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2, MW: 18.02\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Upstream Reactions (Building the Carbon Skeleton and Guanidine)\n\n**Reaction U1: Acetylene + HCN \u2192 Acrylonitrile**\n- Acetylene + Hydrogen cyanide \u2192 Acrylonitrile\n- SMILES: C#N.C#C>>C=CC#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to acetylene, forming the C2-C1 bond. The resulting vinyl nitrile (acrylonitrile) retains both unsaturation and the nitrile electrophilic center for subsequent Michael addition.\n\n**Reaction U2: Acrylonitrile + NH3 \u2192 3-Aminopropanenitrile**\n- Acrylonitrile + Ammonia \u2192 3-Aminopropanenitrile\n- SMILES: C=CC#N.N>>N#CCCN\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile. NH3 adds to the \u03b2-carbon of the conjugated system in a 1,4-fashion, yielding the 3-amino nitrile.\n\n**Reaction U3: 3-Aminopropanenitrile + Cyanamide \u2192 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine**\n- 3-Aminopropanenitrile + Cyanamide \u2192 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine\n- SMILES: N#CCCN.N#CN>>N#CCCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of the primary amine to the nitrile carbon of cyanamide. The amine nitrogen attacks the electrophilic C\u2261N of cyanamide, forming the guanidine (amidine) linkage that becomes arginine's characteristic side-chain functional group.\n\n### Cyclization Reactions (Ring Formation and Opening)\n\n**Reaction C1a: 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine \u2192 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine (dry cyclization)**\n- SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1\n- Agents: **Elemental copper** (Cu) and **hydrogen sulfide** (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Intramolecular cyclization \u2014 the terminal guanidine nitrogen attacks the nitrile carbon, forming the C-N bond that closes the six-membered pyrimidine ring. Water from the medium (or intramolecular) hydrolyzes the resulting imine at C4 to give the hemiaminal (hydroxyl at C4).\n\n**Reaction C1b: 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine + H2O \u2192 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine (hydrolytic)**\n- SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N.O>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1\n- Agents: **Elemental copper** (Cu) and **hydrogen sulfide** (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Same cyclization as C1a with explicit water as co-substrate for nitrile hydrolysis during ring closure.\n\n**Reaction C1c: 2-(2-Cyanoethyl)guanidine + H2O \u2192 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine + NH3**\n- SMILES: N#CCCNC(=N)N.O>>NC1=NCCC(O)N1.N\n- Agents: **Elemental copper** (Cu) and **hydrogen sulfide** (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Same cyclization as C1b with ammonia released as a byproduct of the nitrile-to-hemiaminal conversion.\n\n**Reaction C2: 4-Hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine + HCN \u2192 Amino((3-cyano-3-hydroxypropyl)amino)methaniminium**\n- SMILES: NC1=NCCC(O)N1.C#N>>N#CC(O)CCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Ring-opening by HCN \u2014 cyanide attacks the hemiaminal carbon (C4) of the pyrimidine ring, breaking the C-N bond and opening the ring. The product is a linear \u03b1-cyanohydrin bearing the guanidine side chain. This ring-opening/cyanation effectively extends the carbon chain by one unit while introducing a new nitrile handle for subsequent transformations.\n\n### Thioamide Chemistry (Cyanosulfidic Transformations)\n\n**Reaction T1: Nitrile thiolysis**\n- Amino((3-cyano-3-hydroxypropyl)amino)methaniminium + H2S \u2192 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide\n- SMILES: N#CC(O)CCNC(=N)N.S>>N=C(N)NCCC(O)C(N)=S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: H2S adds across the nitrile triple bond (C\u2261N \u2192 C(=S)NH2). This is the hallmark cyanosulfidic reaction \u2014 sulfide acts as a nucleophile on the nitrile carbon, converting it to a thioamide. The hydroxyl group at the adjacent carbon is preserved.\n\n**Reaction T2: Reductive dehydroxylation**\n- 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide \u2192 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)butanethioamide + H2O\n- SMILES: N=C(N)NCCC(O)C(N)=S>>N=C(N)NCCCC(N)=S.O\n- Agents: **Elemental copper** (Cu) and **hydrogen sulfide** (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Cu/H2S-mediated reductive elimination of the hydroxyl group as water. The thioamide group facilitates this by stabilizing the developing carbocation or radical intermediate at the adjacent carbon. The net result is deoxygenation of the carbon skeleton.\n\n**Reaction T3: Thioamide-to-nitrile conversion**\n- 4-(Diaminomethylideneamino)butanethioamide + H2O + HCN \u2192 2-(4-Cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine + H2S + NH3\n- SMILES: N=C(N)NCCCC(N)=S.O.[C-]#N>>N#CC(O)CCCNC(=N)N.S.N\n- Agents: **Elemental copper** (Cu) and **hydrogen sulfide** (H2S)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Complex exchange reaction \u2014 the thioamide is hydrolyzed/displaced, releasing H2S and NH3, while HCN provides a new nitrile group at the terminal carbon. Water participates in the hydrolysis of the thioamide and hydroxylation of the carbon. This reaction effectively cycles between thioamide and nitrile functional groups, a key feature of cyanosulfidic chemistry that enables carbon chain rearrangement.\n\n### Final Steps (Amination and Hydrolysis to Arginine)\n\n**Reaction F1: Amination**\n- 2-(4-Cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine + NH3 \u2192 2-(4-Amino-4-cyanobutyl)guanidine\n- SMILES: N#CC(O)CCCNC(=N)N.N>>N#CC(N)CCCNC(=N)N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at C4, converting the \u03b1-hydroxynitrile (cyanohydrin) to the \u03b1-aminonitrile. This introduces the \u03b1-amino group that will be present in the final amino acid product. The reaction is thermodynamically driven by the greater stability of the C-N bond relative to the C-O bond in the aminonitrile context.\n\n**Reaction F2: Nitrile hydrolysis to carboxylic acid**\n- 2-(4-Amino-4-cyanobutyl)guanidine + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Arginine + NH3\n- SMILES: N#CC(N)CCCNC(=N)N.O.O>>N.N=C(N)NCCCC(N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Stepwise nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 water attacks the nitrile carbon of the \u03b1-aminonitrile, forming an amide intermediate (\u03b1-aminoamide), which undergoes further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (\u03b1-amino acid) with release of ammonia. This is the same Strecker-type nitrile hydrolysis that produces other amino acids (alanine, serine, glycine) from their respective aminonitrile precursors under aqueous cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following reagents and catalysts are experimentally documented for reactions in the L-arginine synthesis network:\n\n- **Elemental Copper (Cu)**: Metallic copper serves as a reductant and electron-transfer mediator in the cyanosulfidic reaction network. In combination with hydrogen sulfide, it facilitates: (a) intramolecular cyclization of 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine to the pyrimidine ring (Reactions C1a/b/c), (b) reductive dehydroxylation of the hydroxy-thioamide intermediate (Reaction T2), and (c) thioamide-to-nitrile conversion (Reaction T3). Copper is prebiotically plausible \u2014 native copper occurs in hydrothermal and volcanic deposits, and copper sulfides are common in sulfide-rich environments. Used in: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202.\n\n- **Hydrogen Sulfide (H2S)**: A key cyanosulfidic reagent that serves dual roles: (a) as a nucleophile that converts nitrile groups to thioamides (Reaction T1), and (b) as a co-reagent with elemental copper in reductive transformations (Reactions C1a/b/c, T2, T3). H2S is abundantly available in volcanic environments, hydrothermal vents, and early Earth surface waters. It is the sulfur source that drives the characteristic nitrile \u21cc thioamide cycling of cyanosulfidic chemistry. Used in: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN, H2S, acetylene, cyanamide, and other simple molecules can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids through an interconnected reaction network. For arginine specifically, the paper establishes a complete 10-step pathway from acetylene and HCN to arginine via a sequence of nitrile additions, guanidine formation (from cyanamide), pyrimidine cyclization (Cu/H2S-mediated), ring-opening by HCN, thioamide chemistry (H2S thiolysis, Cu-mediated dehydroxylation, thioamide-to-nitrile conversion), amination, and final nitrile hydrolysis. The pathway demonstrates how the guanidinium side chain of arginine \u2014 unique among the proteinogenic amino acids \u2014 arises naturally from cyanamide incorporation into a growing nitrile chain. The same cyanosulfidic framework produces precursors to other amino acids (alanine, serine, glycine), nucleotides, and lipid building blocks, supporting a common chemical origin for all major classes of biomolecules.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary \u2014 Cyanosulfidic)\nThe entire arginine synthesis pathway operates under cyanosulfidic surface conditions as described by Patel et al. (2015):\n- **Geochemical setting**: Evaporitic pools or geothermal fields with access to volcanic outgassing products (HCN, H2S, acetylene) and dissolved minerals (copper).\n- **HCN chemistry**: Hydrogen cyanide is the primary C1 building block, used in Steps 1 (with acetylene), 5 (pyrimidine ring-opening), and 8 (thioamide-to-nitrile conversion). HCN is produced by UV photolysis of N2/CH4 atmospheres and by volcanic/impact processes.\n- **Cyanamide**: The nitrogen source for guanidine formation (Step 3). Cyanamide (H2N-C\u2261N) is produced by UV irradiation of HCN solutions and has been detected in interstellar space.\n- **Cu/H2S redox system**: Elemental copper and hydrogen sulfide work in concert to drive reductive cyclization, dehydroxylation, and thioamide cycling. This Cu/H2S system is a distinctive feature of the Patel et al. cyanosulfidic chemistry.\n- **Acetylene**: The C2 building block for the initial carbon skeleton (Step 1). Acetylene is produced by UV photolysis of methane, volcanic outgassing, and impact shock synthesis.\n- **Wet-dry cycling**: The aqueous hydrolysis steps (Steps 4, 10) and dehydration steps (Step 7) are consistent with evaporitic pool environments where wet-dry cycles concentrate reactants and drive both hydrolysis and condensation reactions.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: H2S and HCN from hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface pools where Cu-mediated cyanosulfidic chemistry occurs.\n2. **Volcanic \u2192 Surface**: Acetylene, HCN, and cyanamide from volcanic/impact sources are deposited into surface water bodies.\n3. **Surface \u2192 Biochemical**: Arginine produced in cyanosulfidic pools enters the prebiotic amino acid inventory, available for peptide bond formation, role as a catalyst/organizer in prebiotic RNA-peptide interactions, and proto-metabolic assembly. Arginine's guanidinium group is particularly important for electrostatic interactions with phosphate groups in nucleic acids.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: The cyanosulfidic pathway produces racemic arginine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Possible mechanisms include enantioselective adsorption on chiral mineral surfaces, amplification by autocatalytic crystallization, or circularly polarized light.\n\n2. **Copper availability**: The pathway requires elemental copper for several key steps (cyclization, dehydroxylation, thioamide conversion). The abundance and accessibility of native copper in early Earth surface environments is debated. Copper sulfides are common in sulfide-rich environments but metallic copper requires reducing conditions.\n\n3. **Multi-step yield**: The 10-step linear pathway means that even moderate yields per step compound to low overall yield. For example, 50% yield per step gives ~0.1% overall yield. Concentration mechanisms (evaporation, mineral adsorption, thermophoresis) are needed to accumulate sufficient product.\n\n4. **Cyanamide concentration**: Step 3 requires cyanamide in sufficient concentration to compete with other electrophiles (HCN, formaldehyde) for reaction with 3-aminopropanenitrile. The prebiotic steady-state concentration of cyanamide relative to other cyanide species is uncertain.\n\n5. **Intermediate stability**: Several intermediates contain reactive functional groups (nitriles, thioamides, cyanohydrins) that could undergo side reactions or decomposition under UV irradiation. The selectivity of the pathway depends on kinetic control and the relative rates of productive vs. destructive reactions.\n\n6. **Guanidine uniqueness**: Arginine is the only proteinogenic amino acid with a guanidinium group. The cyanamide-dependent route to this group is specific \u2014 without cyanamide, the pathway cannot produce arginine. This raises questions about whether cyanamide availability was a bottleneck for arginine incorporation into early protein-like polymers.\n\n7. **Ring-opening selectivity**: Step 5 (HCN opening of the pyrimidine ring) must occur regioselectively at C4. The factors controlling this selectivity (pH, HCN concentration, temperature) and the competing ring-opening products have not been fully characterized.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote